TX-TYPE SEARCH, RESTRUCTURED INTO C'S TWO PHASES: the fix for both headline
mechanisms `callcount_2026-09-04` found, with the before/after call counts that
prove the port now reproduces C's control flow rather than merely its output.

date:   2026-09-04
host:   r7900x (x86_64-linux, AMD Ryzen 9 7900X, 24 cores). valgrind does not
        run on the Apple-Silicon Mac this work was written on.
before: 708ef6386 (this commit's parent)
after:  708ef6386 + the commit this file lands with
C:      reference/svt-av1 @ 3115c0c1, via rust/tools/perf_c_encode. The oracle
        was NOT rebuilt for this chunk — the binary already built in the primary
        checkout was reused, exactly as the campaign brief requires.
tool:   valgrind-3.26.0 --tool=callgrind (Ir only; no cache/branch sim) +
        tools/perf_profile/callcount.py --demangle for per-function totals and
        stock `callgrind_annotate --tree=caller` for caller-specific edges.
grid:   gradient 512x512 qp40, presets 2 / 6 / 10, STILL config, warmup=0 —
        the same three cells callcount_2026-09-04 used, so the numbers join.
timing: benchmarks/perf_ab_txtscreen_2026-09-04.tsv (separate, unniced,
        interleaved paired A/B on the same quiet box).

=================== BYTE-IDENTITY OF THE MEASURED RUNS ===================

All NINE callgrind runs (before/after/C x p2/p6/p10) wrote the same three OBUs:
  p2   5,126 B  sha256 12ac5773eb96ccfa...
  p6   5,995 B  sha256 5eef4a78a80d40b6...
  p10  6,743 B  sha256 923d046b98312cd8...
so before == after == C at every cell, and no count below was taken from a run
whose own output diverged.

=================== WHAT C ACTUALLY DOES (transcribed, with lines) ===================

`tx_type_search`, Source/Lib/Codec/product_coding_loop.c. The per-candidate loop
body is a THREE-gate funnel, and the port implemented only the outermost gate
in C's position:

  :4643-4645  best_cost_tx_search = ~0; dct_dct_cost = ~0;
              best_satd_tx_search = INT_MAX;          <- once per CALL, not
                                                         per tx-type group
  :4646-4648  satd_early_exit_th = only_dct_dct ? 0
                                 : is_inter ? txt_ctrls.satd_early_exit_th_inter
                                            : ...th_intra
  :4650-4657  the qp-based scaling of that threshold
  --- per tx-type group, per tx-type (loops open at :4691-4693) ---
  :4709-4718  GATE 1, the rate gate: skip a type whose signalling rate alone
              already costs more than txt_rate_cost_th/1000 of dct_dct_cost
              (the compare itself is :4714)
  :4729-4740  svt_aom_estimate_transform  <- the CHEAP phase: forward transform
                                             ONLY, into ctx->tx_coeffs
  :4741-4755  GATE 2, THE PROMOTION RULE — verbatim:

                if (satd_early_exit_th) {
                    int satd = svt_aom_satd(&tx_coeffs[txb_1d_offset],
                                            txbwidth * txbheight)
                               << ctx->mds_subres_step;
                    if (satd < best_satd_tx_search) {
                        best_satd_tx_search = satd;
                    } else {
                        if ((satd - best_satd_tx_search) * 100
                            > best_satd_tx_search * satd_early_exit_th)
                            continue;
                    }
                }

  :4757-4776  svt_aom_quantize_inv_quantize  <- the EXPENSIVE phase starts here
  :4779-4781  GATE 3a: a non-DCT type with eob == 0 is not signalable -> continue
  :4784-4906  inverse transform (gated at :4784) + spatial/freq distortion
  :4908-4911  GATE 3b: `early_cost = RDCOST(lambda, 0, dist)`; if it already
              exceeds best_cost_tx_search, `continue` WITHOUT estimating rate
  :4913-4939  coefficient rate (three tiers; the real estimator at :4921)
  :4944-4955  the RD compare; dct_dct_cost is set ONLY inside the winning branch
              (:4947's `if (cost < best_cost_tx_search)`)
  :4957-4969  GATE 4: the absolute coeff/dist early exit that breaks BOTH loops
              (:4965's `best_tx_non_coeff < coeff_th || best_cost < cost_th`)

THE PROMOTION RULE, in words, because it is the whole fix: the SATD is taken
over the coefficients THIS candidate's forward transform just produced (not over
a residual, not over pixels); `best_satd_tx_search` is a RUNNING minimum over
the whole call, initialised once at :4645 and NOT reset per tx-type group, so
the admitted set depends on candidate ORDER; a candidate that beats the running
minimum both updates it and is admitted; a candidate that does not is admitted
anyway unless it is worse by more than `satd_early_exit_th` percent of the
running minimum; and a rejected candidate `continue`s BEFORE the quantizer,
so it never writes eob_txt / y_txb_coeff_bits_txt / txb_full_distortion_txt and
never reaches the RD compare. Ties (satd == best) are admitted, since
`(0) * 100 > best * th` is false for any th >= 0.

DCT_DCT can never be rejected: it is the first candidate of the first group and
faces `best == INT_MAX`, so it always takes the update branch. That is what
guarantees `dct_dct_cost` — GATE 1's denominator — is populated.

=================== IS THE PORT BYTE-IDENTICAL BY CONSTRUCTION OR BY LUCK? ===================

BY CONSTRUCTION, in both directions. This was the question the brief said to
answer by reading, before trusting any gate, and the answer is not "the gates
pass".

The port's OLD shape ran the whole `tx_unit` pipeline for every candidate that
passed GATE 1, then computed the SATD post-hoc (via a `txb_coeff_satd` that
re-derived the residual and the transform) and used it to decide whether to
`continue`. Compare the two orderings:

  C      : G1 -> transform -> G2 -> quantize -> G3a -> dist -> G3b -> rate -> compare
  old port: G1 -> transform -> quantize -> dist -> rate -> G2 -> G3a -> compare
  new port: G1 -> transform -> G2 -> quantize -> dist -> rate -> G3a -> compare

G2's predicate is a pure function of (src, pred, tx_type) and of the running
minimum, and the running minimum is updated in the same order in all three. So
the SET of candidates that reach the RD compare is identical, and their costs
are identical, so the winner is identical. Moving G2 earlier cannot change the
bytes because a G2-rejected candidate had no effect under EITHER shape — its
result was computed and then discarded. That is the sense in which the old code
was correct: it paid for the expensive phase in order to throw it away.

Two smaller checks, both done by reading, both clean:
  * The SATD inputs were already identical. `txb_coeff_satd` zeroed its
    coefficient buffer and ran the same `fwd_txfm2d_dispatch` on the same
    residual (same src/pred/stride/offset arguments) that `tx_unit_inner` runs;
    the bd10 twin likewise. So the screen sees the same numbers from the
    transform it now shares as it did from the transform it used to repeat.
  * The port has NO transcription of GATE 3b. That is not a divergence: RDCOST
    is monotonic in rate, so `RDCOST(l, 0, d) > best` implies
    `RDCOST(l, bits, d) > best` for any bits >= 0, and the candidate loses the
    compare the port does run. Same winner, more work. It is the next
    call-count reduction available here, not a correctness gap — see below.

ONE LATENT BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, in an unreachable path, and it moves TOWARD C. The
old screen always took a DCT of the residual; the new one reads whatever the
transform wrote, which on a coded-lossless 4x4 is the Walsh-Hadamard — which is
what C's SATD reads too, since C's is fed by `svt_aom_estimate_transform`. Both
are unreachable (coded-lossless implies `txt_on == false` implies `only_dct`
implies `satd_early_exit_th == 0` implies no screen at all), so no gate can
witness it either way; the new position is the faithful one.

=================== THE MEASUREMENT ===================

Full table: callcount_txtscreen_2026-09-04.tsv. The four numbers that matter:

1. THE PROMOTED-TRIAL COUNT IS NOW EXACTLY C's, AT BOTH PRESETS WHERE THE
   SCREEN EXISTS. The port's tx-type-search quantize edge (derived as the
   quantizer-dispatch total minus the two positive-control edges, mds1's final
   commit and chroma's) against C's `tx_type_search -> svt_aom_quantize_inv_
   quantize` (direct --tree=caller output):
     p2   488,414 -> 270,415   vs C 270,415   EXACT   (was 1.806x)
     p6    10,537 ->   8,397   vs C   8,397   EXACT   (was 1.255x)
     p10      608 ->     608   vs C     608   already exact; no screen exists
   C's admitted fraction is 55.4 % at p2 and 79.7 % at p6, and the port now
   admits the same candidates rather than the same NUMBER of candidates: the
   two downstream consumers agree to the call as well —
     optimize_b     462,758 -> 260,098  vs C 261,827  (p2); 9,987 -> 7,857 vs
                    C 7,857 EXACT (p6); was 1.767x / 1.271x
     cost_coeffs_txb 478,131 -> 299,116 vs C 304,169  (p2); was 1.572x

2. HEADLINE #2's EDGE IS GONE, NOT MOVED. `detect::txb_coeff_satd ->
   residual::residual_i32` was 484,442 (p2) / 10,193 (p6) / 0 (p10); it is now
   0 everywhere because the function is DELETED. C has no such edge at any
   preset. The redundant forward TRANSFORM went with it (the old screen ran one
   too, which the earlier record did not separate out): the port's
   `fwd_txfm2d_dispatch` total falls 1,080,313 -> 595,871 at p2 against C's
   600,171 `svt_aom_estimate_transform` (0.993x), and at p6 22,133 -> 22,133,
   EXACT.

3. THE p10 CONTROL HOLDS. Every count at p10 is unchanged to the unit, and the
   Ir total moves +0.04 % (codegen noise). TXT search collapses to one candidate
   there on both sides, so the screen is never constructed — exactly the cell
   where this fix should do nothing.

4. INSTRUCTION COUNT: 15,404,105,161 -> 11,788,718,910 at p2, **-23.5 %** of the
   port's entire p2 program total. Port/C 3.482x -> 2.665x. p6 -2.9 %.

5. A SECONDARY EFFECT NOT TARGETED, AND WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE IT VALIDATES
   THE SETUP. A rejected trial now returns before `tx_unit_inner` allocates its
   per-call `qcoeff` and `recon` Vecs, so the port's allocator traffic falls
   with it: malloc+calloc+free 4,355,663 -> 3,483,661 at p2 (-20.0 %), calloc
   alone 1,727,991 -> 1,291,992 (-25.2 %), `__memset_avx2_unaligned_erms`
   3,870,785 -> 2,559,654 (-33.9 %). The BEFORE total, 4,355,663, reproduces
   callcount_2026-09-04's allocator figure to the unit at a different commit —
   an independent check that the two measurement setups agree. This does not
   close that finding: C's allocator traffic is still ~2,600 calls, flat across
   presets, and the port's is still 1,340x it.

TIMING (benchmarks/perf_ab_txtscreen_2026-09-04.tsv, unniced interleaved paired
A/B, 9 rounds, same box while it was quiet, ident=Y on every cell):
  512x512 p2  652.54 ms -> 491.28 ms   1.325x faster  (p25/p75 0.747/0.757)
  256x256 p2  294.50 ms -> 216.99 ms   1.353x
   64x64  p2   28.92 ms ->  20.64 ms   1.486x
  p6 1.004x / 1.068x / 1.034x; p10 0.989x / 0.996x / 1.000x (the control).

=================== WHAT IS NOT FIXED, AND IS NOW MEASURABLE ===================

* THE RESIDUAL IS STILL DERIVED PER TX-TYPE TRIAL. C computes it ONCE per
  (tx-depth, TXB) in `perform_tx_partitioning` and every tx-type candidate in
  `tx_type_search` transforms that ONE buffer (`cand_bf->residual`); the port's
  `tx_unit` still starts from src/pred. Post-fix p2: port `residual_i32`
  595,871 vs C `svt_residual_kernel8bit_avx2` 435,245 (1.369x, 160,626 excess
  calls). At p6 the port is already BELOW C (22,133 vs 23,358), so this is a
  p2-shaped opportunity only. Hoisting it means an optional pre-computed-
  residual argument to `tx_unit`, which is a separate commit.
* The port's residual kernel is also ~3.6x C's Ir PER CALL (304,998,509 Ir /
  595,871 calls vs 62,480,484 / 435,245). That is a kernel-speed finding, not a
  call-count one, and this chunk did not touch it.
* GATE 3b (C's `early_cost` pre-rate screen) is still absent. C admits 270,415
  candidates to quantize at p2 but only 253,033 to `svt_av1_cost_coeffs_txb`
  (`--tree=caller`); the port runs the rate estimate on all 270,415. Byte-inert
  by the monotonicity argument above; worth roughly the 6.4 % of
  cost_coeffs_txb's inclusive cost that C skips.
* GATE 4 (the absolute coeff/dist early exit at :4957-4969) has no port
  transcription either. It is INERT on this grid — C's trial width and the
  port's matched exactly BEFORE this fix (488,414 = 488,414 at p2, 10,537 =
  10,537 at p6), which it could not do if C were breaking out early — but that
  is an observation about this content and these presets, not a proof.
* Everything outside gradient / 512x512 / qp40 / still / {p2,p6,p10}. The
  BYTE-identity evidence is far broader (see the commit message); the CALL-COUNT
  evidence is these three cells.

=================== CORRECTIONS TO THE PRIOR RECORD ===================

* `callcount_2026-09-04`'s HEADLINE #1 says the fix should drop "the
  `txt_search -> tx_unit` edge" to C's promoted count. That edge is UNCHANGED at
  591,253 (all callers) and it should be: the screen lives INSIDE the unit, at
  C's position between the transform and the quantizer, so a rejected trial
  still enters `tx_unit_inner` — it just returns before the expensive phase.
  The C-comparable quantity is the quantizer edge, which is what the table
  above reports and what is now exact. A future session re-checking this must
  not read the flat `tx_unit_inner` count as "the fix did not land".
* HEADLINE #2 counts only the redundant RESIDUAL (484,442 calls, 237,923,278
  Ir). The old post-hoc screen also ran a redundant forward TRANSFORM on every
  one of those trials, which is the larger half of what it cost; the transform
  row in the table separates them.
* The prior record's p2 Ir total (15,403,051,340 at c4ff4727e) reproduces here
  as 15,404,105,161 at 708ef6386 — 0.007 % apart across five commits, which is
  a useful cross-check that the two measurement setups agree.
